  "changelog": "v168: focus duplicate curation (ADR 0010) \u2014 the owner's vault carried duplicate focuses/ideas (an exact-name-modulo-case \"fear\"/\"Fear\" class, and token-variant pairs like \"Betty Jo\" vs \"Betty Jo Taylor\"); three layers now stop them, in order. (1) Door guards: every focus-creation path (roadmap.focus_new, the roadmap CLI's add, derive_focuses) now refuses to create a focus whose normalized name collides with an existing one \u2014 derive_focuses folds a same-key case-variant pair into one focus instead of materializing a twin; the other doors point at the existing focus and refuse. The normalization (lowercase, slugify, strip a leading \"the \") is one shared function (lifehug_core.normalized_focus_key), reused by entity_roster's existing alias-matching instead of a second copy. (2) Roster fold: recommend_focuses.recommend() now folds detected-entity stats through each type's settled roster aliases before scoring, so two pending ideas the roster already knows are one entity emerge as a single recommendation with summed evidence. (3) A new AI interaction, interactions/focus_curation/, judges the residue neither deterministic layer can resolve on its own \u2014 first-encounter near-name pairs like \"Betty Jo\"/\"Betty Jo Taylor\" \u2014 emitting merge/map/keep verdicts with no reason field (owner decision: no reason capture, anywhere) that system/focus_curation.py applies deterministically; absent AI, the roster fold remains the floor, never a deterministic merge guess. A new read-only command, focus-dupes --report, lists all three duplicate classes (certain focus collisions, near-name pairs, folding pending ideas) for the owner's own cleanup \u2014 detection only, this PR does not merge existing duplicates. See ADR 0010.",
